lesson: 213
title: A pair for each person
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: Each person has two feet and two hands, so each person wears a pair: 2 shoes, 2 boots, 2 gloves.
explain: First count the people, then take 2 things for each person.
explain: 3 children and 2 grown-ups leave their boots by the door: 3 + 2 = 5 people, and 5 × 2 = 10 boots.
vars: a=name c=2..9 g=2..4 z=2..8
answer: (c + g) * 2
exercise: {c} children and {g} grown-ups come in from the rain and leave their boots by the door. How many boots are by the door?
exercise: At {a}'s party {c} children and {g} parents take off their shoes in the hall. How many shoes are in the hall?
exercise: {g} parents and {c} children put on wellies to walk in the mud. How many wellies do they wear?
exercise: It is snowing. {c} children and {g} teachers go out, each wearing gloves. How many gloves are they wearing?
exercise: {c} pupils and {g} teachers go into the mosque and leave their shoes at the door. How many shoes are at the door?
exercise: How many mittens do {c} children and {g} grown-ups wear when everyone has mittens on?
exercise: {c} kids + {g} grownups, shoes off at the door. how many shoes is that
exercise: we're {c} kids n {g} parents, all need wellies for the farm. how many wellies
exercise: {a}'s mum says take shoes off pls — {c} kids came, oh and {g} mums. how many shoes on the mat now
exercise: {c+1} kids — no, {c} kids, {a} went home — and {g} grownups, everyone in gloves. how many gloves
exercise: {a} reckons {c} kids and {g} adults is {c+g} shoes. wrong innit, how many shoes
exercise: {z} dogs ran in too lol but {c} kids n {g} grownups left boots at the door. how many boots
test: Everybody in a group of {g} grown-ups and {c} children takes off a pair of shoes. Count the shoes.
test: How many boots stand in the hall when {c} children and {g} adults have each taken theirs off?
test: The {c} children on the trip and their {g} helpers all pull on gloves. What number of gloves is that?
test: {a} lines up everyone's wellies after the walk: {g} grown-ups' and {c} children's. How many wellies does {a.he} line up?
test: shoes off for {c} kids n {g} adults. how many shoes
test: {g} grownups n {c} kids, {z} umbrellas by the door too, everyone's boots off. boots?
test: if {c} children and {g} grown-ups all put on mittens, how many mittens are being worn?
exam: Given that {c} children and {g} adults each deposit their footwear at the entrance, determine the total number of shoes so deposited.
exam: It is stipulated that every member of a party of {c.words} children and {g.words} adults shall wear gloves. Ascertain the number of gloves required.
exam: A group comprising {g} adults and {c} minors removes its boots prior to admission. Compute the aggregate number of boots.
exam: State the number of wellington boots worn by a party of {c} pupils accompanied by {g} adults, each person being shod in wellington boots.
exercise: {g+1} grown-ups were going on the walk with {c} children, but Uncle Joe was "under the weather" and stayed at home. Everyone who goes needs a pair of wellies. How many boots are needed?
exercise: dad got laid off so we're skint, gotta get wellies off the market. {c} kids n {g} grownups, a pair each. how many boots, numpty
test: Gran calls it "resting her eyes", but she is fast asleep on the sofa with her slippers on. Everyone else, {c} children and {g} other grown-ups, leaves a pair of shoes at the door. How many shoes are at the door?
